    "growth_divergence_warning": "Equity 3yr CAGR 5.7% diverges >30% from revenue 3yr CAGR 9.4%; 5yr equity/revenue CAGRs (13.1%/13.8%) are inflated by the stock-funded Cantel acquisition (FY2022). EPS 3yr CAGR omitted: FY2023 GAAP EPS $1.07 is depressed by a ~$490M goodwill impairment (Q2 FY23), making it meaningless.",
    "notes": "FCF CAGRs are on the owner-FCF series (reported FCF - SBC - equity-award taxes): FY2021 $414.8M, FY2023 $342.5M, FY2026 $898.2M. The 3yr figure starts from a FY2023 trough (Cantel integration, supply-chain inventory build, high capex) and is not a forward rate. Owner-FCF CAGR is close to reported, so SBC is not growing faster than cash flow (SBC ~1.0-1.3% of revenue)."
  },
  "scenario_narratives": {
    "base": "STERIS keeps compounding as a recurring-revenue infection-prevention franchise (consumables + service ~70% of revenue): surgical procedure volumes drive Healthcare consumables and service, AST contract sterilization grows with med-device volumes, Life Sciences recovers from the post-COVID bioprocessing destock. Organic growth fades from ~7% to 3%; margins expand ~2pp from pricing and mix; capex normalises after the AST capacity programme.",
    "bull": "The radiation-modality shift away from ethylene oxide and med-device outsourcing make AST a high-single-digit grower with pricing power, bioprocessing restocking lifts Life Sciences, and the hospital capital-equipment replacement backlog persists. Growth stays 6-8% for five years and adjusted EBITDA margin reaches ~31.5%.",
    "bear": "The axes turn against it at once: EPA EtO rules and Willowbrook-style litigation raise AST cost, US hospital capex budgets are cut as Medicaid funding tightens, tariffs squeeze consumable margins, and single-use endoscopes erode the Cantel reprocessing franchise. Growth slips to 2.5-4% and margin falls to 27%."
  },
  "valuation_philosophy": {
    "summary": "Owner-FCF view of a mature infection-prevention compounder: SBC and equity-award taxes expensed, share count flat (funded by buybacks), EBITA-based tax so book amortization of Cantel intangibles creates no phantom shield, and a terminal cap of 18x base so the value does not lean on a Gordon multiple the market need not grant.",
    "key_risks_to_valuation": [
      "Ethylene-oxide regulation and litigation in Applied Sterilization Technologies",
      "Hospital capital-equipment budget cycle and US healthcare funding changes",
      "Tariffs on imported components/consumables compressing margin",
      "Single-use endoscope substitution eroding the Cantel reprocessing franchise",
      "Terminal value ~60% of EV; value is sensitive to the 8.5% WACC and 18x cap"
    ]
  },
