    "wacc_rationale": "USD flows, USD rate. Risk-free ~4.3% (10y UST), beta ~0.85 (defensive healthcare consumables/services), ERP 5.0% -> cost of equity ~8.6%. Debt ~$1.9B against ~$20.5B market cap (~8.5% weight) at ~4.5% pre-tax / ~3.5% after tax -> WACC ~8.2%; rounded up to 8.5% for the capital-equipment cyclicality inside Healthcare. Same WACC in every scenario -- risk is carried by the scenario paths and weights, not a stacked premium.",
    "lease_treatment": "US GAAP: operating-lease cost sits inside operating income/EBITDA, so leases are charged in the flows; operating-lease liabilities are NOT in net debt (one treatment).",
    "nci_treatment": "Non-controlling interests immaterial (<$5M); ignored.",
    "notes": "Model: owner-FCF component DCF (operating company: infection-prevention consumables, service, capital equipment, contract sterilization AST and Life Sciences). Routed per dcf-methods SKILL.md; first valuation of STE (no memory entry). Irish plc but US-domiciled operations, USD statements and USD NYSE quote, so the non-US adaptations do not apply. D&A path is DEPRECIATION ONLY (FY26 $218.3M = 3.7% of revenue): the $268.2M amortization of acquired intangibles (mostly Cantel, 2021) is excluded so EBIT here is EBITA, and the cash-tax rate is calibrated to that base (FY26 cash taxes $289.0M / EBITA ~$1,370M = 21.1%; ~22% unlevered after adding back the interest shield). This avoids a perpetual tax shield from book-only amortization. Adjusted EBITDA = GAAP operating income + D&A + SBC (FY26 $1,650.0M, 27.8%); GAAP OI still carries restructuring/acquisition costs, so the margin base is slightly conservative. Net debt at 2026-06-30 = long-term debt $1,650.2M + short-term borrowings $243.4M - cash $482.3M (the CSV TotalDebt column omits the short-term borrowings). Working capital capture negative (-8% of the revenue increase): receivables and inventory exceed payables in a capital-equipment/consumables manufacturer. Interest income not stripped (net debt, not net cash). Revenue CAGRs are inflated by the Cantel acquisition (closed June 2021, paid largely in stock) and depressed by the Dental divestiture; the forward path is anchored to organic growth (FY26 +8.7% reported, Q1 FY27 +7.3% YoY). No Analysis.json existed at build time, so narratives are drawn from the filings and business profile. FY2027 numeric guidance was not in the extracted data; year-1 growth is anchored to Q1 FY27 run-rate.",
